{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n33 \n \n \nmixtures other than air.]  Such nitrogen exchange has enabled the production of the flames that \nare soot-free at all flow conditions, i.e., which are \u2018permanently blue.\u2019 \n \nThe Flame Design experiment involves gaseous hydrocarbon diffusion flames supported on a \nporous spherical burner.  Soot inception limits and flame extinction limits are obtained as \nfunctions of the nitrogen exchange, flow direction, and pressure.  Extensive analytical and \ncomputational modeling support the experimental investigation, where they are facilitated by \nthe spherical flame shape enabled by the microgravity environment.  Models include gas-phase \nand burner-surface radiation. \n \nBuoyant gravity-driven flow complicates the interpretation of the existing results and has led to \ndifferent theories to explain the permanently-blue diffusion flames.  It is not known whether this \nphenomenon is due more to a change in the direction of convection across the flame, or to a \nchange in the detailed structure of the flame. \n \nThe challenges of normal-gravity studies are avoided by performing the tests on the International \nSpace Station (ISS), where the non-buoyant microgravity flames allow the effects of convection \ndirection and flame structure to be independently varied.  By ejecting fuel from the burner into \nan oxidizer (e.g., air) in a buoyancy free system, the convective flow across the flame is \nunambiguously from fuel to oxidizer. Alternatively, by ejecting oxidizer from the burner into an \natmosphere of fuel, the flow is from oxidizer to fuel. \n \nFurthermore, microgravity studies of flames allow for a range of simplifications and provide \nvarious advantages over normal-gravity studies.  For example, the flicker of flame, which is a \nbuoyancy-driven (i.e., gravity-driven) instability, is eliminated yielding quasi-steady flames in \nmicrogravity.  Length scales are increased in microgravity flames facilitating analysis of the flame \nstructure.  Momentum-dominated flames, which are important for most practical combustion, \ncan be studied at low velocities to simplify analysis.  Microgravity flames tend to have a much \nstronger sensitivity to their atmosphere and exhibit a much broader range of characteristics than \nnormal-gravity flames because of the near absence of buoyant entrainment.  The long residence \ntimes in microgravity flames can lead to strong soot production, but many microgravity flames", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 33}}